From aeef05db4ee076127614c3eb80c66f38fd699b43 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: nuk3s <44327449+nuk3s@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 02:08:55 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] dsv41: a restore or a resume the pool has no room for waits
 for room instead of a prefill from 0

With the spill tier on (SPILL_GIB), a disk restore that the KV pool could not place while streams ran started no
read: rank 0 logged "reading 565248 kept tokens from disk did not start (ContextFull); prefilling" and ran the prompt
from 0. With KV sharding on (KV_SHARD=1), a memory hit that its part could not keep was dropped the same way: "the
kept state of 567296 tokens at 1452032 was not resumed (no room in its part); the prompt runs from 0". A prompt pass
in the same place gets its room: kept states give theirs, a live stream moves to the other part, or the pass waits at
the queue's front until a stream ends. On a pair serving agent traffic, one conversation of about 600K tokens ran from
0 fourteen times in 36 hours this way (4 restores, 10 hits), a fifth of the prompt tokens computed in that time.

A restore and a resume now get room in the same ways:

- lane_host (SPILL_GIB, with KV_SHARD on or off): a restore refused with ContextFull while streams run goes back to
  the queue's front as a refused prompt pass does (full_wait, full_retry_ns) and asks for the restore again at its
  next admission. It waits TF_SPILL_LOAD_WAIT_MS (30 s) at most from its first refusal, then it is admitted on what
  memory holds, as before. The bound matters because the restore needs room in its point's recorded part, while a
  prefill from 0 may take any part. With no stream active it is admitted at once, as before. A waiter cancelled at
  the queue's front releases full_wait at once. New counters restores_room_waits and restores_room_wait_expired
  (tensorfold:prompt_cache_* on /metrics), and one log line a request for each.
- parts, kv_lanes (KV_SHARD=1): when evictions in a restore's part cannot free its rows, planRestore moves one live
  stream out of that part (planRelocate, with its guards and its choice), and Kv.restore runs the move and plans
  again, as Kv.place does for an admission.
- parts (KV_SHARD=1 with carries on, the default): a hit that would be dropped resumes in place instead. The kept
  states over its tail give their room, one live stream there moves to the other part, or the request waits for the
  streams there on lanes' held_wait_ns clock (10 s); then the hit is dropped as before. Only for states of carry_min
  tokens or more, inside the part, never over held, pinned or read rows, and never while a live stream holds the
  state itself.

A request that waits for room holds the queue, as a waiting prompt pass does: nothing behind it is admitted until it
fits, a second passes and it asks again, or its bound (30 s for a restore, 10 s for a hit) runs out. No switch.

Tests: four host tests in lane_host_robust_test.zig, each also run with the prompt pass in pieces and cut-ins
(fill_chunks 1, cut_in 8192); two in kv_lanes.zig (rank 0's and the follower's planners agree, the move's transfer
runs on both); an "11 room" section in parts.zig with three new mutants (hit_drops_for_room, hit_no_wait,
restore_no_relocate), each killed by its own check. "9 twin: its rows are no room" makes its live stream unmovable
(moving it would make the room now), and the "10 random pools" sweep checks the new outcome (carry off drops the hit,
carry on keeps it) under "11 room". The pieces runs turn the service fence's debug panic off: on this base the run
loop asks for parked work's service while a pass stands between two pieces, and the fence refuses it (a release
build counts and skips it).
